Exercise 2 In this exercise, you must implement the power iteration method to find the largest (in absolute value) eigenvalue and the corresponding eigenvector of a given matrix. As an input, you will receive a square matrix and the number of iterations. As an output, return the eigenvalue and eigenvector found with the power iteration method.
<jupyter_code>
def power_iteration(A, num_iter):
    
    # Initial guess
    b_k = np.random.rand(A.shape[1])
    
    for _ in range(num_iter):
        # Normalize b_k vector
        b_k /= np.linalg.norm(b_k)
        # Calculate the next iterate (matrix-by-vector multiplication)
        b_k = A @ b_k
    
    # At this point, b_k is an approximation for the eigenvector
    # You must normalize it before returning
    b_k /= np.linalg.norm(b_k)
    
    # Approximation for the eigenvalue is the dot product of the initial vector and the result
    lambda_k = b_k @ A @ b_k

    return lambda_k, b_k
