This is a simple and direct proof of the spectral theorem which ensures that any square matrix has an orthonormal eigenbasis (i.e., the eigenvectors are mutually orthogonal and normalize to unit length). A matrix $A$ with eigenvalues $\lambda_i$ and eigenvectors $v_i$ can be diagonalized as $A = V \Lambda V^{-1}$, where $\Lambda$ is a diagonal matrix containing the eigenvalues, and $V$ is the matrix of eigenvectors. If we rearrange these two components, we get $A = V^{-1} \Lambda V$, which shows that the eigenvectors and eigenvalues are well-defined. The crucial step is the fact that $V^{-1}$ is the transpose of $V$. We also assert that $Av_i = \lambda_i v_i$, which, given that the eigenvectors are orthonormal, forces $Av_i = \lambda_i v_i$.
